# 11 Scan substring: (*scs:(ref) pattern)
# https://www.pcre.org/current/doc/html/pcre2pattern.html
#
# Needs PCRE2 10.45 or newer.
#
# What it is: a zero-width assertion that runs a sub-pattern against the
# CONTENT of a captured group, as if that capture were its own subject.
# ^ and $ inside it anchor to the capture's boundaries and not the real
# subject's. The capture can be referenced by number (*scs:(1)...) or by
# name (*scs:('name')... or (<name>)...).
#
# What it enables: "the captured field must itself satisfy this pattern"
# checks inside one match. The field gets validated as a whole right after
# being captured, with no second preg call in code.

# Expect: 0: 123;
#         1: 123
# The field before ';' is captured and then asserted to be ALL digits.
# The ^ and $ inside (*scs:) bind to the capture "123" and not to the
# subject.
/(\w+);(*scs:(1)^\d+$)/
    123;x

# Expect: No match
# "12a" fails the all-digits assertion, and no way of re-splitting the
# subject can satisfy it.
/(\w+);(*scs:(1)^\d+$)/
    12a;x

# Expect: 0: 1;
#         1: 1
# The assertion participates in backtracking. \w+ first grabs "abc1" and
# the assertion holds (it needs a digit in the capture, referenced by name
# here). But the engine also needs ';' next, so \w+ shrinks until the rest
# of the subject fits. The final capture is "1", the smallest \w+ tail
# that still holds a digit and is followed by ';'.
/(?<w>\w+);(*scs:(<w>)\d)/
    abc1;x
